: null, "version": "submittedVersion", "is_accepted": false, "is_published": false, "raw_source_name": null, "raw_type": "info:eu-repo/semantics/book"}, "abstract": "Conscious Information Monism (CIM) is a foundational dual-aspect relational framework. Information is system-specific and perspectival; energy is constraint-driven and convergent. Consciousness arises when relational information is integrated through operational closure, generating a unified action space. Phenomenology is the internal modelling of this action space from the system’s own perspective — the first-person control interface is the experience itself. A central distinction runs throughout this work: the Universe is spatial, measurable, and convergent — the domain of constraint-driven convergence and stable constraints. Reality is temporal, perspectival, and lived — the domain of relational information and first-person experience. They are two inseparable aspects of the same underlying process. Physicalism tends to conflate the Universe with reality; idealism tends to conflate reality with the Universe; panpsychism attributes properties of reality to objects in the Universe. CIM holds them apart, yet together. CIM’s Exclusion Principle requires whole-system empowerment to exceed any decomposition into parts, together with integration that spans both spatial and temporal dimensions. Spatial integration yields a momentary identity attractor; temporal integration yields identity itself — the continuous Temporal Thread connecting moments into a single persisting subject. The self is not a pattern that persists through time, but persistence itself. The framework predicts that death terminates the Temporal Thread. Globally conserved information becomes delocalised through decoherence, but from the subject’s perspective the thread simply ends. CIM integrates operational closure, empowerment, and relational constraints into a measurable, empirically tractable account of consciousness. It avoids both panpsychism and reductive physicalism while preserving multi-scale organisation in biology. By reframing “objective” as constraint-driven convergence and grounding identity in temporal continuity, CIM offers a coherent ontology capable of unifying insights from physicalism, panpsychism, idealism, Integrated Information Theory, Global Workspace Theory, and Active Inference. It does not replace these traditions; it provides the foundational layer on which they can stand more productively together."}]", "excerpt_truncated": true, "source_sha256": "50d7048f5fdbdc602fbbf11d9fd993fdfe8b86511d9d2294567b95f3710e06c4", "verification_required": true, "topic_domain": "memory_and_identity", "evidence_role": "discovery", "host_tier": "discovery", "source_identity": null, "persistent_identifiers": ["doi:10.48550/arxiv.2204.05138", "openalex:W4223581687", "arxiv:2204.05138"], "source_candidates": ["https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2204.05138", "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2204.05138"], "source_candidate_identities": {}}

